AIL can provide much higher mapping resolution. Is R/qtl2 capable of make genetic maps using the AIL population? Or can we treat the AIL as F2 and use qtl1 to make a map, then use it in qtl2?

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Tue Jul 16 15:35:23 2024
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Tue, 16 Jul 2024 15:35:23 +0000
Subject: Re: AIL genetic map construction
Message-ID: <IuVonauIGH0.rWwR1DAuAAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <IuVonauIGH0.SXU3bCaUAgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <IuVonauIGH0.SXU3bCaUAgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/IuVonauIGH0/m/rWwR1DAuAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Genetic map estimation has not been implemented for AILs in R/qtl2.

I agree that you could treat it as an F2 and estimate the map, either in R/qtl1 or R/qtl2, but you'd need to rescale it, as the intervals would be expanded due to the multiple generations of recombination.

karl

